<<~ ahu slot=`#/ci-triage-read-2-lane-lease-end-e2e-shard-3-4-fa`>>

! CI triage read 2 — lane lease-end, E2E shard 3/4 failed TWICE

Mask: CI-Reader (Tasked Spirit), read-only. Handback to final.md in your seat dir.
Attachments (absolute paths): this run's log /home/joshu/.lares-scratch/lease-end/seats/ci-triage-2/e2e-shard3.log (head 5ba18c736); the first run's log /home/joshu/.lares-scratch/lease-end/seats/ci-triage/e2e-shard3.log (head 3b9cac44d) and its triage /home/joshu/.lares-scratch/lease-end/seats/ci-triage/final.md (which read the failure as an unrelated load flake in tests/e2e/boot-holds-the-store.test.ts, with no assertion in the log).
Lane worktree: /home/joshu/.lares-scratch/lease-end/wt; lane diff from the span: `git -C /home/joshu/.lares-scratch/lease-end/wt diff --stat 5c3d032dc 5ba18c736` (mesh device-delegation; keyhive face grant verifier, join writer, seat, operator-daemon-behavior founder-standing wiring).
Job: (1) Which test(s) fail this time, with the first assertion or error quoted? The same test as before, or another? (2) A second failure on the same shard voids a flake call unless the evidence shows otherwise: does the lane's founder-standing wiring (the daemon join reading founderDyad, the seat's founderStanding port, a grant refusing as pending) plausibly make a boot or store-ownership e2e hang or fail? Trace from the failing test into the lane's changed code. (3) Does the span's own recent CI pass this shard (check whether the test sits in a heavy roster or carries a known flake note in tools/)? Close with CAUSED / FLAKE / UNKNOWN and the next measure (for example: run the named e2e file locally on the lane head vs the span tree, 600 s cap).

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/seat-law`>>

!! SEAT LAW (appended by tools/launch-codex.mjs to every brief; it binds this seat)
* A lane launches no Sol seat; the controller's one Sol read per train runs at the read floor of 0%.
* Stay inside the wall. Approval never escalates; a build seat has no network, loopback included; only a research seat takes `--search`. A step that needs more than the wall stops and names itself in `out.md`; the wall holds by refusal.
* Never commit and never run a git write. The lead commits each red, then each fix.
* Never build (`pnpm build`), never run vitest browser mode, never stand a vessel, socket or daemon. Typecheck (`tsc -p … --noEmit`) and run single test files that open no socket; mark any test that needs a socket "lead runs".
* A "stale build" from the dist guard stops the run; name it, and the lead builds.
* A read seat writes no file: its final message carries the hand-back. Any other seat writes `out.md` and every note in the seat directory alone. `-o` names `final.md`; stdin closes (`< /dev/null`); `TMPDIR` and `TMPPREFIX` sit inside the seat directory.
* Never run the battery; the lead holds its lock. Never read a credential file.
* Launch without `--ephemeral`: each seat keeps its rollout, so the spend tally counts it.
* The lead runs `pnpm build` in its worktree after every span merge and before each build seat; a verdict read off a stale dist counts as unread.

<<~/ahu>>

